import type { Section } from "./types";

export const article = {
  id: `WHQT0pon4ct`,
  title: `Miraculous: Tales of Ladybug & Cat Noir`,
  description: `A comprehensive guide to the universe of Miraculous: Tales of Ladybug & Cat Noir—tracing Thomas Astruc's original concept, the lore of Kwamis and Miraculous jewels, the evolution across seven seasons, and its global animated franchise.`,
  thumbnail: "data:image/webp;base64,",
  category: `Animation`,
  tags: [`Miraculous`, `Ladybug`, `Cat Noir`, `Marinette Dupain-Cheng`, `Adrien Agreste`, `Hawk Moth`, `Kwamis`, `Tikki`, `Plagg`, `Thomas Astruc`, `Jeremy Zag`, `Miracle Box`, `Paris`, `Miraculous World`],
  content: [
    { type: "p", text: `In Paris, the person you pass by every day might be the very same hero standing right beside you when the city faces great peril... without you ever knowing it. This is the paradox lived by **Marinette Dupain-Cheng** and **Adrien Agreste**; behind their everyday routines and their heroic alter egos lies a secret that neither of them knows about the other.` },
    { type: "p", text: `When danger strikes, Marinette becomes **Ladybug**, and Adrien transforms into **Cat Noir**. Together, they embark on adventures to protect Paris, while each tries to balance their ordinary life with their secret responsibility. From this premise, which blends superheroes with everyday life and secrets, begins the world of **Miraculous: Tales of Ladybug & Cat Noir**.` },
    { type: "h2", text: `What is Miraculous: Tales of Ladybug & Cat Noir?` },
    { type: "p", text: `**Miraculous: Tales of Ladybug & Cat Noir** is a 3D animated series rendered in **3D CGI**, blending superhero atmosphere, adventure, comedy, drama, romance, and fantasy. Its events are primarily set in the French city of **Paris**, where two teenagers lead lives that appear ordinary on the surface, yet carry an entirely different responsibility whenever the city is threatened.` },
    { type: "image", src: "data:image/webp;base64,", alt: "Ladybug and Cat Noir, the series' central heroes, featured on an official poster.", caption: "Ladybug and Cat Noir, the series' central heroes, featured on an official poster." },
    { type: "p", text: `Those two teenagers are **Marinette Dupain-Cheng** and **Adrien Agreste**. In their daily lives, they face issues familiar to any teenager: from schoolwork and social relationships to personal challenges. But when Paris needs protection, they assume two heroic identities: Marinette becomes **Ladybug**, and Adrien becomes **Cat Noir**.` },
    { type: "p", text: `The core idea lies not merely in having two distinct identities, but in the secret that separates them. Ladybug and Cat Noir work together as partners in defending the city, yet neither of them knows the true identity of the person standing behind the other's mask. This makes their relationship in everyday life different from their dynamic during adventures, giving the story room for comedic, dramatic, and romantic situations alongside battles.` },
    { type: "p", text: `It is important to clarify a point regarding the work's classification: although some of its designs and concepts might remind viewers of anime, **Miraculous** is not a Japanese anime from a production standpoint. It is a French-produced animated series based on 3D CGI animation, with the participation of companies and studios from various countries, including Japanese and South Korean entities. Therefore, the presence of a Japanese company within its development and production background does not change its production origin or the nature of its style.` },
    { type: "h2", text: `How Was the Idea of Ladybug Born?` },
    { type: "p", text: `Before Ladybug became a heroine recognized by audiences of the series, she was merely a small concept that began with a ladybug drawing on a colleague's t-shirt. Behind this beginning stands **Thomas Astruc**, who is considered the pivotal figure in the inception of the heroine's concept and the universe that later evolved around her.` },
    { type: "p", text: `In an interview with the newspaper **Le Figaro** dated November 2, 2017, Astruc spoke about the first inspiration behind the character. He had been interested in superheroes since childhood, and while working on an animation project, he met a colleague wearing a t-shirt with a ladybug illustration. The two began exchanging small sketches on sticky notes, and Astruc drew a concept of a superheroine inspired by that insect.` },
    { type: "p", text: `He was fond of the character he drew, and it did not stop at simply designing a heroine in a ladybug-themed suit. He began thinking about a wider universe surrounding her, featuring other heroes, characters, rules, powers, and a narrative that could extend far beyond the idea of a single character.` },
    { type: "h3", text: `From Comic Books to a Television Project` },
    { type: "p", text: `Interestingly, the initial conception of the project was not originally intended as a television series. Astruc was considering presenting the idea in the form of **Comics**—graphic novels—within a superhero universe with a French flavor.` },
    { type: "image", src: "data:image/webp;base64,", alt: "An early concept sketch of Ladybug from when the project was originally conceived as a comic book.", caption: "An early concept sketch of Ladybug from when the project was originally conceived as a comic book." },
    { type: "p", text: `However, producer **Jeremy Zag** saw greater potential in the preliminary concepts and suggested expanding the project to include a television series and cinematic works, rather than remaining confined to comic books. Thus, the idea gradually began transforming from a heroic character inspired by a simple sketch into an entertainment project possessing an expansive storytelling universe.` },
    { type: "p", text: `It is important here to distinguish between Astruc's account of the inception of the Ladybug character and the broader creative credits of the work; official materials cite, alongside his name, both **Jeremy Zag** and **Nathanaël Bronn** among the names associated with the creation of the franchise.` },
    { type: "p", text: `There was also a different conception of the work's visual appearance at an early stage. The studio **Toei Animation** produced a short three-minute test using **2D** traditional animation. Ultimately, however, the project headed toward a **3D CGI** animation style, a format that became an essential part of its well-known visual identity.` },
    { type: "image", src: "data:image/webp;base64,", alt: "Frames from the early 2D anime test pilot before the production transitioned to 3D CGI.", caption: "Frames from the early 2D anime test pilot before the production transitioned to 3D CGI." },
    { type: "h3", text: `Why Paris Became the Home of the Story` },
    { type: "p", text: `Paris was not the only option Astruc considered while developing the concept. He mentioned that he initially thought of **Hong Kong**, but Jeremy Zag preferred setting the events in **Paris**, given the city's global fame and the romantic atmosphere associated with it in people's minds.` },
    { type: "image", src: "data:image/webp;base64,", alt: "Paris as depicted in the series; the iconic city serving as the stage for the heroes' adventures.", caption: "Paris as depicted in the series; the iconic city serving as the stage for the heroes' adventures." },
    { type: "p", text: `This choice gave the work a clear, easily recognizable backdrop; instead of the heroine's adventures taking place in an entirely fictional city, Paris's streets and landmarks became part of the environment in which the heroes move and strive to protect.` },
    { type: "h3", text: `A Project Bringing Together France, South Korea, and Japan` },
    { type: "p", text: `The production of Miraculous was not the result of a single studio working within one country; rather, it relied on international cooperation from its early stages. According to a statement by South Korea's **EBS** network issued on July 22, 2015, companies from three countries participated in the project: from France, the companies **Zagtoon** and **Method Animation**; from South Korea, the studio **SAMG Animation**; and from Japan, the company **Toei Animation**.` },
    { type: "p", text: `The statement also noted the participation of South Korean entities in broadcasting and distribution, including EBS and SK Broadband. This background illustrates how the concept moved from a creative vision that began with a small doodle to an animated production co-developed by entities from more than one country.` },
    { type: "p", text: `This journey demonstrates that the identity viewers know today was not present from the very first sketch; rather, it was shaped through stages of reconceptualization, development, and international cooperation.` },
    { type: "h2", text: `Two Characters, Two Different Lives: Marinette and Adrien` },
    { type: "p", text: `The world of **Miraculous** does not rely on a single heroine, but on two characters, each having their own life and distinct way of interacting with the world. What makes their dynamic intriguing is that transforming into a hero does not merely grant them a new suit and superpowers; it reveals a side of their personalities that does not manifest in the same way in their daily lives.` },
    { type: "image", src: "data:image/webp;base64,", alt: "Marinette and Adrien in their civilian daily lives, away from their superhero masks.", caption: "Marinette and Adrien in their civilian daily lives, away from their superhero masks." },
    { type: "h3", text: `Marinette Dupain-Cheng: From Student to Paris's Heroine` },
    { type: "p", text: `In her daily life, **Marinette Dupain-Cheng** is a student trying to balance her studies, social relationships, and personal aspirations. She has a clear passion for fashion and design, and this aspect forms an important part of her identity outside the life of superheroes.` },
    { type: "p", text: `However, when she becomes **Ladybug**, the nature of the responsibilities she faces changes. She does not merely participate in battles; she assumes a leadership role in protecting Paris, and needs to make decisions and handle challenging situations during confrontations. Here, a significant contrast emerges between her ordinary life and her heroic life: the girl who deals with teenage challenges also becomes responsible for decisions that can affect the safety of the entire city.` },
    { type: "image", src: "data:image/webp;base64,", alt: "Marinette's transformation sequence into Ladybug.", caption: "Marinette's transformation sequence into Ladybug." },
    { type: "p", text: `She is accompanied by **Tikki**, a magical entity known as a *Kwami*. Tikki helps her transform into Ladybug, and her power is linked to the concept of **Creation**, which is one of the fundamental concepts in the Miraculous lore that we will explore shortly.` },
    { type: "p", text: `As the story progresses, Marinette's responsibility is no longer limited to being a heroine protecting the city; it expands to encompass the role of **Guardian**, the guardian of the Miraculouses. This shift increases her responsibilities and adds a new dimension to her character and trajectory within the work.` },
    { type: "h3", text: `Adrien Agreste: A Life Under Expectations and a Freer Persona` },
    { type: "p", text: `On the other hand, **Adrien Agreste** leads a different life. He works as a fashion model for his father's brand, but he also lives under the weight of his father's strict expectations, which leaves his daily life governed by constraints he does not always choose himself.` },
    { type: "p", text: `Hence comes the significance of his heroic identity, **Cat Noir**. When Adrien transforms into Cat Noir, he finds greater space to express himself compared to what his customary life permits. Therefore, this identity does not represent merely a means to combat adversaries; it grants him an opportunity to showcase a different side of his personality and pursue a higher degree of freedom and independence.` },
    { type: "image", src: "data:image/webp;base64,", alt: "Cat Noir: the heroic persona in which Adrien discovers greater freedom to express himself.", caption: "Cat Noir: the heroic persona in which Adrien discovers greater freedom to express himself." },
    { type: "p", text: `He is accompanied by **Plagg**, the Kwami bound to him, who helps him transform into Cat Noir. Plagg's power is linked to the concept of **Destruction**. Among the charming details that add a lighthearted touch to their relationship is that Plagg is well known for his intense love of cheese; this is an amusing comedic trait that contrasts with the nature of the destructive force he represents.` },
    { type: "h3", text: `Crossed Feelings... and Identities Unknown to Their Bearers` },
    { type: "p", text: `In the early stages of the story, Marinette harbored feelings for Adrien, while Cat Noir expressed his admiration for Ladybug. Here arises a paradox that goes beyond mere masks: the person Marinette loves is her superhero partner, yet the relationship between them does not unfold in the way an outside observer might expect.` },
    { type: "image", src: "data:image/webp;base64,", alt: "Ladybug and Cat Noir atop the rooftops of Paris: partners unaware of each other's true identity.", caption: "Ladybug and Cat Noir atop the rooftops of Paris: partners unaware of each other's true identity." },
    { type: "p", text: `At that time, Adrien was unaware of the reality of Marinette's feelings, and Ladybug did not know that Cat Noir was Adrien himself. Thus, each of them found themselves facing a different relationship depending on the identity through which they interacted with the other.` },
    { type: "p", text: `This duality gives the series room to blend romance, comedy, and drama. The two might behave in one manner in their daily lives, and then meet in their heroic roles with an entirely different dynamic emerging between them. The viewer, unlike the characters, can see both pictures at once; this is what makes the situations resulting from the secret so compelling.` },
    { type: "p", text: `However, this dynamic characterizes the early stages of the story and is not a permanent description of their relationship up to the latest seasons; the relationship developed later on, which becomes distinctly apparent in the events of Season Seven.` },
    { type: "h3", text: `Gabriel Agreste: The Adversary Behind the Public Image` },
    { type: "p", text: `The conflict takes on a more complex dimension with **Gabriel Agreste**, Adrien's father. In his public life, Gabriel is renowned as a famous fashion designer, but behind this public facade, he bears the identity of **Hawk Moth**, one of the foremost antagonists in the foundational stages of the series.` },
    { type: "image", src: "data:image/webp;base64,", alt: "Gabriel Agreste and his clandestine alter ego, Hawk Moth.", caption: "Gabriel Agreste and his clandestine alter ego, Hawk Moth." },
    { type: "p", text: `His primary motivation throughout his story is his attempt to bring back his wife. To achieve his goal, he uses the **Miraculous** associated with the butterfly, releasing magical butterflies known as **Akumas** to influence ordinary people and transform them into adversaries endowed with superhuman abilities.` },
    { type: "p", text: `Here lies his difference from an antagonist who relies on confronting heroes directly each time; he is able to make other individuals part of his threat, creating recurring and renewed challenges for Ladybug and Cat Noir.` },
    { type: "p", text: `However, it is important to place this character in his proper chronological context. **Hawk Moth** is not the name that describes the antagonist across all phases of the series; Gabriel's role and identity evolve as events progress, and new developments later emerge surrounding the wielder of the Butterfly Miraculous. Therefore, it is more accurate to view it as the primary identity of an antagonist during a crucial phase of the narrative, not as a permanent description of everything that follows.` },
    { type: "h3", text: `The Heroes Do Not Stop at the Duo` },
    { type: "p", text: `With the expansion of the show's world, protecting Paris does not remain solely the responsibility of Ladybug and Cat Noir. Other heroic figures emerge who wield Miraculouses and participate in broadening the circle of heroes, including **Rena Rouge**, **Carapace**, **Bunnyx**, **Viperion**, and **Ryûko**.` },
    { type: "image", src: "data:image/webp;base64,", alt: "The allied circle of heroes widens alongside Ladybug and Cat Noir.", caption: "The allied circle of heroes widens alongside Ladybug and Cat Noir." },
    { type: "p", text: `The presence of these characters opens the door to relationships, alliances, and situations that the duo alone could not provide; it also illustrates that the world of heroes is larger than two individuals and their powers. Nevertheless, understanding the core premise does not require reviewing every single hero or enumerating their capabilities; what matters most here is recognizing that the circle of protection expands as the story evolves.` },
    { type: "h2", text: `What is the Secret Behind the Miraculous and the Kwamis?` },
    { type: "p", text: `After learning about the characters who shoulder heroic responsibility, an important question arises: where do these abilities come from in the first place? At the heart of the show's fictional universe lie magical relics known as **Miraculous**, linked to magical entities called **Kwamis**. Through this bond, a significant part of the heroes and powers system in the work is formed.` },
    { type: "h3", text: `Magical Relics Behind the Transformations` },
    { type: "p", text: `A **Miraculous** is a magical piece of jewelry or talisman that can bond with a wielder, granting them the ability to transform into a hero and wield a superhuman power. These relics appear in various forms, such as the earrings worn by Ladybug or the ring worn by Cat Noir.` },
    { type: "p", text: `However, the magical relic alone does not create this connection; each Miraculous is linked to a magical creature known as a **Kwami**. These entities assist the Miraculous wielders in transforming and channeling their abilities, and each Kwami is associated with a distinct concept or power.` },
    { type: "image", src: "data:image/webp;base64,", alt: "A gathering of Kwamis; each celestial creature embodies a fundamental concept and power.", caption: "A gathering of Kwamis; each celestial creature embodies a fundamental concept and power." },
    { type: "p", text: `This system can be understood through the two examples we have already encountered: **Tikki** is the Kwami linked to Ladybug's earrings and represents the power of **Creation**, while **Plagg** is the Kwami linked to Cat Noir's ring and represents the power of **Destruction**.` },
    { type: "p", text: `In this way, Marinette's relationship with Tikki or Adrien's with Plagg is not merely a relationship between a person and a companion creature; it is part of the source of power that allows each of them to assume their heroic identity.` },
    { type: "h3", text: `Creation and Destruction: Two Opposing Forces` },
    { type: "p", text: `The choice of Creation and Destruction holds greater significance than merely giving the two heroes distinct abilities. They represent two opposing concepts within the universe of the series: **Creation** as the force of bringing forth, and **Destruction** as the force of tearing down.` },
    { type: "p", text: `This opposition provides the power system with a central, easily grasped core idea: one side is associated with creating things, and the other is associated with destroying them. From here stems the profound importance of the two Miraculouses together; the concept of uniting them is linked to one of the primary drivers of conflict in the early seasons.` },
    { type: "p", text: `Thus, the difference between Ladybug's power and Cat Noir's power is not simply a visual detail or a way to diversify combat scenes, but a fundamental part of the fictional premise upon which the world and the conflict between its heroes and adversaries are constructed.` },
    { type: "h3", text: `The Miracle Box and the Guardian's Responsibility` },
    { type: "p", text: `At first, it might appear that the Miraculous system is restricted solely to the pieces associated with Ladybug and Cat Noir, but the picture widens as the narrative unfolds. There are other Miraculouses, other Kwamis, and a broader realm of power that extends well beyond the two main protagonists.` },
    { type: "p", text: `Here enters the **Miracle Box**, an essential cornerstone of this world's structure, housing a collection of Miraculouses. Its existence demonstrates that the magical powers and relics are not disparate elements introduced merely to give each character a unique gimmick; rather, they belong to a broader system requiring protection and stewardship.` },
    { type: "image", src: "data:image/webp;base64,", alt: "The Miracle Box, the ancient chest housing the foundational set of Miraculous jewels.", caption: "The Miracle Box, the ancient chest housing the foundational set of Miraculous jewels." },
    { type: "p", text: `Tied to this system is the responsibility of the **Guardian**, the protector tasked with guarding the Miraculouses and safeguarding this realm. As events develop, Marinette herself becomes bound to this role, expanding her duty beyond using her Miraculous in combat into a much larger responsibility toward the entire order to which these relics belong.` },
    { type: "p", text: `This transition adds new gravitas to her journey; she is no longer treating her power merely as a means to confront imminent danger in the city, but has also become responsible for protecting the universe of the Miraculous itself.` },
    { type: "h3", text: `A System Opening the Door to a Broader World` },
    { type: "p", text: `The strength of this system is that it gives the series room to expand without remaining confined to two heroes and two abilities. The presence of multiple Miraculouses and distinct Kwamis allows new power-wielders to emerge, adding fresh possibilities for forming superhero teams and diversifying encounters.` },
    { type: "p", text: `At the same time, the role of Guardian makes the world more interconnected; every Miraculous is part of a coherent framework carrying weight and responsibility, not just a convenient tool a hero picks up when in need of extra strength.` },
    { type: "p", text: `In this manner, the Miraculous lore unites the thrilling spectacle of transformations and superhuman feats with a narrative foundation that facilitates the expansion of the Miraculous universe and the deepening of its ongoing conflicts.` },
    { type: "h2", text: `How Are Villains Created, and Why Are Their Powers Tied to Emotions?` },
    { type: "p", text: `One of the aspects that gives the confrontations in **Miraculous** their distinctive flavor is that a new adversary does not necessarily emerge from someone who was evil from the outset. Often, the story begins with an ordinary individual facing a personal dilemma or difficult circumstance, whose emotions then become the catalyst for a superhuman crisis. To understand this dynamic, we must examine the process known as **Akumatization**.` },
    { type: "h3", text: `When Emotions Turn into a Superhuman Threat` },
    { type: "p", text: `The process of **Akumatization** is defined as the method by which an ordinary person is transformed into an adversary possessing superhuman abilities. It begins with the use of an **Akuma**, a magical butterfly linked to the Butterfly Miraculous, utilized by its wielder to influence a chosen target.` },
    { type: "image", src: "data:image/webp;base64,", alt: "An Akuma butterfly poised before seeking out a vulnerable host.", caption: "An Akuma butterfly poised before seeking out a vulnerable host." },
    { type: "p", text: `When the process succeeds, that individual gains a superpower that turns them into a new adversary confronting Ladybug and Cat Noir. In this way, the wielder of the Butterfly Miraculous is able to generate renewed threats from people who were living entirely normal lives before entering this confrontation.` },
    { type: "p", text: `Yet what distinguishes this concept is not merely the existence of a magical butterfly that turns people into adversaries, but the intimate connection between the power granted to each person and their emotional state—the exact reason that brought them to that breaking point.` },
    { type: "h3", text: `Not Every Confrontation Begins with a New Villain Concept` },
    { type: "p", text: `Behind a person's transformation may lie feelings of anger, jealousy, fear, or frustration. Rather than being an arbitrary power given to a new character, the ability is almost always directly tied to the grievance they are experiencing and the way they react to it.` },
    { type: "p", text: `Imagine someone feeling misunderstood by those around them, or someone furious over a situation they perceive as deeply unjust. In the world of Miraculous, such feelings can become the starting point for a new adversary, with their emotional distress reflected in the nature of the power they receive. Thus, a personal problem evolves into a larger threat, and the superhuman battle becomes an extension of what originated in everyday life.` },
    { type: "p", text: `**Thomas Astruc** spoke about this approach to building episodes in his interview with *Le Figaro* on November 2, 2017. He explained that writing a compelling story in Miraculous does not start with inventing a new villain, but starts from the emotion and how it is dealt with. From a character's reaction to a specific situation, the concept of a superpower symbolizing the emotion they are going through can emerge.` },
    { type: "p", text: `This explains why certain confrontations carry significance that extends beyond the battle itself. An adversary is not merely an obstacle appearing so heroes can fight another battle; they can be a reflection of a relatable human issue, such as jealousy, anger, or feeling let down. By translating these emotions into superhuman abilities, the series links its fantastical events to circumstances that viewers can genuinely comprehend and empathize with.` },
    { type: "p", text: `This premise lends the episodes evident variety; even when the broad outlines of a confrontation are similar, the underlying dilemma behind each adversary differs, and with it, the nature of the power and threat. It also underscores the importance of the everyday side of the narrative, because what transpires between characters outside of battles can itself become the inception of the danger they will face later on.` },
    { type: "h3", text: `The Conflict is Bigger Than Hawk Moth` },
    { type: "p", text: `In the foundational stages of the series, the Akumatization process was linked predominantly to **Hawk Moth**, who used the Butterfly Miraculous to fashion adversaries to challenge the heroes. However, it is a mistake to reduce all threats in Miraculous to this single character.` },
    { type: "p", text: `As the narrative advanced, the nature of the conflict broadened, introducing new alliances, roles, and antagonists. An example of this is **Mayura**, who became a dangerous ally to Hawk Moth at a later stage, whose presence helped complicate the conflict rather than leaving it restricted to one foe.` },
    { type: "image", src: "data:image/webp;base64,", alt: "Mayura, the formidable ally whose arrival intensified the conflict.", caption: "Mayura, the formidable ally whose arrival intensified the conflict." },
    { type: "p", text: `Threats then evolved as the story transitioned through its various phases, introducing new Miraculous wielders and expanding the allied superhero roster. In Season Six, the name **Monarch** comes to the fore as he nears the realization of his objective, while Season Seven introduces a new wielder of the Butterfly Miraculous known as **Chrysalis**. This antagonist can take on the physical appearance of other people, introducing an element of doubt regarding identity and trust into the conflict.` },
    { type: "p", text: `Consequently, the central question at each stage is no longer limited to: who will be turned into an adversary this time? Rather, the conflict also becomes tied to who orchestrates the threat, what has shifted in the balance of power, and how the heroes will navigate a universe that has grown vastly larger and more intricate.` },
    { type: "h2", text: `How Did the Show's World Evolve Across the Seasons?` },
    { type: "p", text: `Certain Miraculous episodes may appear as self-contained adventures: a problem arises, a person is transformed into an adversary, and the heroes engage in a new confrontation. Yet following the series reveals that behind this episodic rhythm lies an overarching narrative that develops incrementally, deeply impacting characters, relationships, and the nature of the conflict.` },
    { type: "image", src: "data:image/webp;base64,", alt: "Season 1", caption: "Season 1" },
    { type: "image", src: "data:image/webp;base64,", alt: "Season 2", caption: "Season 2" },
    { type: "image", src: "data:image/webp;base64,", alt: "Season 3", caption: "Season 3" },
    { type: "image", src: "data:image/webp;base64,", alt: "Season 4", caption: "Season 4" },
    { type: "image", src: "data:image/webp;base64,", alt: "Season 5", caption: "Season 5" },
    { type: "h3", text: `The Adventure of Each Episode... and a Continually Advancing Narrative` },
    { type: "p", text: `The storytelling methodology of Miraculous can be understood through two concurrent tracks.` },
    { type: "p", text: `The first is the episodic adventure track: events begin with a dilemma or circumstance experienced by an individual, their emotions may lead to Akumatization, and the heroes then confront the new adversary and strive to halt them. This structure provides each episode with a distinct problem and a unique confrontation.` },
    { type: "p", text: `The second track is the serialized narrative spanning across seasons. Here, the repercussions of events do not vanish with the conclusion of an episode; rather, characters continue to mature, relationships shift, new secrets are unveiled, Ladybug's responsibilities expand, and the antagonists and objectives driving the conflict transform.` },
    { type: "p", text: `Thomas Astruc noted in his interview with *Le Figaro* that an episode blends an aspect akin to a social comedy focusing on characters and their dynamics, and then shifts into the superhero action realm. He also spoke about constructing compelling characters whose journeys can be tracked across multiple seasons, with an overarching narrative roadmap that preserves space for creative development during writing.` },
    { type: "p", text: `This synthesis makes the series well suited to multiple viewing habits: an audience member can enjoy the standalone adventure of an individual episode, while concurrently following the overarching secrets and cumulative shifts that gather momentum across the story.` },
    { type: "h3", text: `From Early Adventures to an Expanded Universe` },
    { type: "p", text: `**Season One — Establishing the World of Heroes**` },
    { type: "p", text: `Season One commenced by introducing Marinette, Adrien, and their heroic alter egos, laying the foundation for the struggle against Hawk Moth. The focal point during this phase was acquainting viewers with the characters and the duality of their daily lives, while establishing the confrontation pattern that would become a hallmark of the series.` },
    { type: "p", text: `**Season Two — Expanding Relationships and Lore**` },
    { type: "p", text: `Following the establishment of the core premise, the world began to widen. Additional details emerged concerning the Miraculous system, creating broader room for character development and relationship dynamics. The narrative no longer relied solely on presenting the heroes and the primary antagonist, but began unveiling wider horizons for the universe in which they dwell.` },
    { type: "p", text: `**Season Three — The Emergence of the Guardian's Responsibility**` },
    { type: "p", text: `Season Three represents an important milestone in expanding the Miraculous system; viewers discover the existence of other Miraculouses and new power-wielders, while the role of **Guardian** becomes much more defined through Marinette's meeting with the Guardian and her discovery of the vastness of this lore.` },
    { type: "p", text: `This development is vital because it shifts the scale of responsibility borne by the heroes. The story is no longer solely about confronting a villain threatening Paris, but encompasses a wider realm requiring guardianship and governance.` },
    { type: "p", text: `**Season Four — A Team of Heroes and More Complex Adversaries**` },
    { type: "p", text: `In Season Four, the hero roster expands with the arrival of new powers and allies assisting Ladybug and Cat Noir in their battles. Concurrently, the threat grows in complexity with the rise of **Mayura** as a formidable ally to Hawk Moth.` },
    { type: "p", text: `Thus, the conflict no longer hinged on a clash between two heroes and a solitary foe; there emerged a broader team of champions facing a significantly more perilous alliance on the adversarial side.` },
    { type: "p", text: `**Season Five — Escalation of the Conflict and Major Turning Points**` },
    { type: "p", text: `In Season Five, the story reaches a heightened pitch of escalation. The adversary becomes more powerful and relentless, bringing fundamental shifts to the trajectory of the conflict and the world built over preceding seasons.` },
    { type: "p", text: `It is best to view this phase as a watershed moment in the conflict rather than merely another set of recurring adventures; preceding events begin yielding consequences that reshape the trajectory of the narrative.` },
    { type: "p", text: `**Season Six — A World After the Loss of the Miracle Box**` },
    { type: "p", text: `Season Six does not start from the same baseline where previous phases concluded. According to official synopses, Ladybug faces a challenging phase following the loss of the **Miracle Box**, while Monarch nears the realization of his objective.` },
    { type: "p", text: `At the same time, the heroes must navigate the fallout of these developments and collaborate under altered circumstances. This phase illustrates how a major disruption to a foundational element of the world reverberates across the challenges faced by the characters.` },
    { type: "p", text: `**Season Seven — New Responsibilities and a Different Threat**` },
    { type: "p", text: `Season Seven continues broadening the narrative through a phase where Ladybug has become the Guardian of the Miraculouses, leading a new team of wielders. The season introduces a fresh antagonist in **Chrysalis**, the new wielder of the Butterfly Miraculous, who possesses the ability to adopt the appearance of other people.` },
    { type: "image", src: "data:image/webp;base64,", alt: "Ladybug in a promotional visual for Season Seven.", caption: "Ladybug in a promotional visual for Season Seven." },
    { type: "p", text: `The season also centers upon the choices confronting Marinette and their impact on her daily life and heroic responsibilities, alongside more intricate relationships, novel abilities, and challenges that test the partnership between Ladybug and Cat Noir.` },
    { type: "p", text: `The relationship between Marinette and Adrien witnesses significant maturation compared to early stages; the official Season Seven synopsis describes them as attempting to build a normal relationship, yet the secret Marinette harbors regarding Adrien's father prevents her from being entirely candid with him. Thus, the irony of unshared feelings and secret identities that defined the beginning no longer encapsulates their dynamic at this juncture.` },
    { type: "h3", text: `Season Seven: Staggered Release Dates Across the Globe` },
    { type: "p", text: `Season Seven consists of **26 episodes**, yet its rollout did not occur on a single synchronized global date for all viewers. The official production entity announced on October 28, 2026, a premiere timetable distributed across various channels, platforms, and territories.` },
    { type: "p", text: `Broadcasts commenced in Brazil on the Globo network on October 30, 2026, followed by France on TF1 on November 1. In Europe, the Middle East, and Africa (EMEA), the season premiere on Disney Channel was set for November 14, 2026. For the United States and Latin America, the premiere on Disney Channel was scheduled for November 20, with availability on Disney+ starting on November 21 in those regions.` },
    { type: "p", text: `Consequently, as of November 14, 2026, the season had already commenced in Brazil and France, while reaching its announced premiere date in the EMEA region on that same day, whereas release dates for the United States, Latin America, and Disney+ remained slated for later.` },
    { type: "p", text: `This distinction is crucial when discussing Season Seven; it is inaccurate to describe October 30, November 14, or November 21 as its single universal global release date. They represent regional rollout windows and distinct platform premieres within a comprehensive distribution schedule.` },
    { type: "p", text: `Thus, Season Seven mirrors the ongoing evolution of the Miraculous universe, featuring an expanded roster, greater stakes, and new adversaries, while consistently maintaining the balance between the characters' personal journeys and the crises endangering Paris.` },
    { type: "h2", text: `How Did Miraculous Become a Global Phenomenon?` },
    { type: "p", text: `**Miraculous: Tales of Ladybug & Cat Noir** did not remain merely a series whose adventures unfolded solely on the streets of Paris. Over the years, its footprint expanded to reach audiences across various regions of the globe, branching into digital channels, special releases, and brand-new projects. Published figures illustrate the magnitude of this proliferation, while the intrinsic nature of the work helps elucidate some of the elements that account for audiences' sustained engagement.` },
    { type: "h3", text: `From Paris to Over 150 Territories Worldwide` },
    { type: "p", text: `According to data published by **Miraculous Corp** in May 2026, the series has reached over **150 territories worldwide**. This figure reflects the extensive scope of its distribution beyond its domestic market and its presence across a multitude of broadcast channels and streaming platforms.` },
    { type: "image", src: "data:image/webp;base64,", alt: "Official 10th-anniversary celebration poster uniting heroes across the Miraculous franchise.", caption: "Official 10th-anniversary celebration poster uniting heroes across the Miraculous franchise." },
    { type: "p", text: `Reaching this number of territories does not necessarily imply that all viewers follow the series in the exact same manner or at the exact same time; broadcast schedules and episode availability vary significantly between markets. Nonetheless, it clearly establishes that Miraculous has become a production with broad international resonance, rather than an effort aimed at the audience of a single nation.` },
    { type: "h3", text: `A Digital Presence Extending Far Beyond Television Episodes` },
    { type: "p", text: `This widespread reach also extended to YouTube, where official Miraculous channels have become a cornerstone of the franchise's digital footprint.` },
    { type: "p", text: `In an announcement published in October 2026, it was noted that the franchise's YouTube channels had surpassed approximately **54 million subscribers**, accumulating more than **55 billion lifetime views**.` },
    { type: "p", text: `However, it is crucial to understand what these metrics measure: they encompass the channels of the **Miraculous franchise** and its varied digital content, and are not strictly attributable to a single channel or the main television series alone. Furthermore, cumulative views do not equal an identical number of individual viewers; a single person may view multiple clips, and repeat viewings of the same clip are common.` },
    { type: "p", text: `Therefore, while both figures offer a compelling snapshot of the sheer scale of the project's digital presence, they do not by themselves reveal the exact number of unique individuals who have watched the television series.` },
    { type: "h3", text: `What Do Parrot Analytics Findings Reveal?` },
    { type: "p", text: `Among the most notable indicators illustrating audience engagement with the series are the findings published by **Parrot Analytics** on October 7, 2026. Miraculous achieved remarkable results in global audience demand measurement over a 90-day window ending on September 18, 2026 (spanning June 21 to September 18).` },
    { type: "p", text: `During this window, the series ranked **First Globally** among all measured children's programs, outperforming over 850 shows aimed at preschool and school-age audiences. It also ranked **Fourth Globally** among all television titles measured by the firm within a comprehensive dataset exceeding 65,000 titles, recording an audience demand level equivalent to **58.2 times the average demand for a television title**.` },
    { type: "p", text: `Yet, what does the demand metric signify in this context?` },
    { type: "p", text: `Parrot Analytics does not measure raw episode stream counts or the direct tally of individuals watching; rather, it utilizes an audience demand index reflecting engagement, buzz, and consumption relative to the average television title. Therefore, achieving 58.2× demand does not imply achieving 58.2 times the viewership numbers, nor is it accurate to describe it as the fourth most-watched series in the world based solely on this report.` },
    { type: "p", text: `The accurate description is that it ranked fourth globally according to the firm's demand index, and first among children's titles included in the study. This is an essential distinction so that robust metrics are not translated into claims divergent from what the source empirically established.` },
    { type: "h3", text: `What Makes the World of Miraculous Appealing to Audiences?` },
    { type: "p", text: `While the aforementioned metrics prove wide distribution and high demand during a defined period, they do not prove that any single factor is solely responsible for this success. Nevertheless, several core elements of the work can be examined as probable contributors toward understanding its broad appeal.` },
    { type: "p", text: `**Dual identities** grant the narrative an easily grasped hook, while concurrently opening an expansive realm for comedic misunderstandings and dramatic situations. The two characters the viewer follows in everyday life are the very same figures seen during superhero exploits, with their behavior shifting depending on the identity in which they appear.` },
    { type: "p", text: `The **romantic dynamic and the identity secret** add another layer of suspense; the viewer knows what the characters do not know about each other, creating moments that blend comedy, romance, and drama, and giving the central relationship room to evolve across the overarching narrative.` },
    { type: "p", text: `Furthermore, the **expandable power framework** enables the introduction of new Miraculous wielders, heroes, and adversaries without needing to overhaul the foundational premise each time. This grants creators room to introduce varied characters and confrontations while safeguarding the world's distinct identity.` },
    { type: "p", text: `The **visual character design** aids in distinguishing the heroes and recognizing them instantly, particularly through distinctive color palettes, costumes, and symbolic motifs associated with each persona. This is complemented by the blend of **comedy, romance, and adventure**, which provides multiple narrative entry points for viewers rather than relying upon a solitary genre.` },
    { type: "p", text: `Finally, the universe of Miraculous does not stop at the core series; the presence of feature films, television specials, and spin-off projects provides opportunities to showcase diverse locales, characters, and formats. This modularity ensures the franchise can connect with audiences through various storytelling mediums, rather than through episodic television alone.` },
    { type: "p", text: `These elements represent an analytical appraisal of the work's creative construction, rather than an empirical study proving that any single element alone generated success. Yet they provide meaningful insight into how Miraculous managed to unite an instantly recognizable concept with a universe capable of continuous expansion.` },
    { type: "h2", text: `The World of Miraculous Beyond the Series` },
    { type: "p", text: `The television series is no longer the sole medium through which the world of **Miraculous** can be experienced. The franchise has broadened to encompass cinematic feature films, television specials, short-form content, and spin-off initiatives offering distinct narrative experiences.` },
    { type: "p", text: `However, not all of these productions represent a direct continuation of the main series' continuity; some re-imagine the premise in an alternate format, others expand the destinations visited by the heroes, while certain projects introduce brand-new worlds and characters altogether.` },
    { type: "h3", text: `Miraculous: Ladybug & Cat Noir – The Movie` },
    { type: "p", text: `**Miraculous: Ladybug & Cat Noir – The Movie** offers a feature-length cinematic adaptation of the show's universe, directed by **Jeremy Zag**, with a screenplay co-written alongside **Bettina López Mendoza**.` },
    { type: "image", src: "data:image/webp;base64,", alt: "Official poster for Miraculous: Ladybug & Cat Noir – The Movie.", caption: "Official poster for Miraculous: Ladybug & Cat Noir – The Movie." },
    { type: "p", text: `The film translates the core characters and foundational premise into an independent cinematic journey, harmonizing action, comedy, romance, and musical spectacle, presenting Ladybug and Cat Noir's origins and arc within a unified feature film format rather than serialized television installments.` },
    { type: "p", text: `It is important not to treat the film as an additional season of the series. It is a standalone cinematic adaptation, and viewers should not assume that its events occupy the exact same continuity as the television series, or that every development within it automatically applies to the primary series narrative.` },
    { type: "h3", text: `Miraculous World Specials: Adventures Beyond Paris` },
    { type: "p", text: `Despite the series' indelible connection to Paris, the **Miraculous World Specials** broaden horizons by staging adventures in distinct international settings, presenting characters and stakes that transcend the customary environment of the show.` },
    { type: "p", text: `In **New York**, the heroes encounter American superheroes and confront a novel threat. In **Shanghai**, Ladybug's journey to the city leads to deeper mysteries and fresh allies. The **London** adventure revolves around time travel and identity revelations, while the **Tokyo** adventure lays the groundwork for introducing a new Japanese team linked to the Stellar Force project.` },
    { type: "image", src: "data:image/webp;base64,", alt: "Miraculous World: New York – United HeroeZ", caption: "Miraculous World: New York – United HeroeZ" },
    { type: "image", src: "data:image/webp;base64,", alt: "Miraculous World: Shanghai – The Legend of Ladydragon", caption: "Miraculous World: Shanghai – The Legend of Ladydragon" },
    { type: "image", src: "data:image/webp;base64,", alt: "Miraculous World: London – At the Edge of Time", caption: "Miraculous World: London – At the Edge of Time" },
    { type: "p", text: `Meanwhile, **Paris – Tales of Shadybug & Claw Noir** presents an alternate-dimension vision of the same world, showcasing alternate versions of Ladybug and Cat Noir where the customary roles between heroes and adversaries are flipped.` },
    { type: "image", src: "data:image/webp;base64,", alt: "Shadybug and Claw Noir: alternate-universe counterparts of the heroic duo.", caption: "Shadybug and Claw Noir: alternate-universe counterparts of the heroic duo." },
    { type: "p", text: `The significance of these specials lies in their expansion of the fictional geography, granting characters room for escapades beyond Paris. They also allow the introduction of new champions, cultures, and settings without necessitating a complete overhaul of the flagship series.` },
    { type: "h3", text: `Miraculous Chibi: Miniature Adventures with a Comedic Slant` },
    { type: "p", text: `**Miraculous Chibi** showcases the characters rendered in Chibi stylization—a simplified aesthetic featuring miniature bodies and comparatively enlarged heads, accompanied by exaggerated expressions and motions suited for comedy.` },
    { type: "image", src: "data:image/webp;base64,", alt: "The heroes reimagined in the simplified, comedic Miraculous Chibi style.", caption: "The heroes reimagined in the simplified, comedic Miraculous Chibi style." },
    { type: "p", text: `In contrast to the primary series, which balances high adventure, drama, and narrative development, this project concentrates on lighthearted daily scenarios and slapstick misunderstandings. The objective here is not driving overarching plotlines forward, but showcasing beloved figures in brief, entertaining comedic vignettes.` },
    { type: "p", text: `A second season was announced in the format of **52 shorts, each running approximately three minutes**, with its rollout commencing on November 28, 2025. Thus, Chibi stands as an established standalone project offering a tangential experience separate from the core narrative, rather than an installment within the mainline seasonal continuity.` },
    { type: "h3", text: `Miraculous Stellar Force: A New Team in Tokyo` },
    { type: "p", text: `Among the most prominent announced expansions within the Miraculous universe is **Miraculous Stellar Force**, a spin-off series introducing a brand-new superhero ensemble set in **Tokyo**, rather than following the Paris-based roster.` },
    { type: "p", text: `The project diverges visually from the flagship series: it utilizes hand-drawn **2D** traditional animation inspired by Japanese anime aesthetics, blending action, comedy, and martial arts sensibilities.` },
    { type: "image", src: "data:image/webp;base64,", alt: "The new Stellar Force superhero team introduced in Tokyo.", caption: "The new Stellar Force superhero team introduced in Tokyo." },
    { type: "p", text: `Its premise centers upon a fresh group of international school students in Tokyo who uncover their connection to the **Stellar Matrix**, an ancient celestial weapon shattered into fragments. Through this roster, the project introduces new characters, mythologies, and threats into the broader Miraculous universe.` },
    { type: "p", text: `The team was introduced initially in the television special **Miraculous World: Tokyo Stellar Force**, which serves as a prelude to this new corner of the world. The spin-off series itself was announced to comprise **26 episodes**, each running approximately 22 minutes, with a planned premiere in **2027** across Disney Channel and Disney+.` },
    { type: "p", text: `Here, one must distinguish between the two entities: Tokyo Stellar Force is a television special that introduces and previews the team, whereas Miraculous Stellar Force is the planned full-fledged spin-off series. It is not Season Eight of the main series; rather, it is an independent venture featuring an entirely separate team, setting, and visual aesthetic.` },
    { type: "h3", text: `Live-Action Series: Miraculous Transitions to Live-Action` },
    { type: "p", text: `In October 2026, the development of the first **Live-Action** television adaptation of the Miraculous universe was announced, established as a partnership between **Miraculous Corp** and **See-Saw Films**.` },
    { type: "p", text: `**Emme Hoy** was appointed to pen the adaptation, as part of an initiative aimed at delivering a live-action version targeting a broader, older audience demographic, while retaining the foundational cornerstones familiar to longtime franchise followers.` },
    { type: "p", text: `This endeavor represents a distinct format departure from animation, shifting the world into a medium powered by real actors rather than animated character models. However, the verified announcement classifies it as an active development project, and has not yet established an official broadcast date or an announced cast roster.` },
    { type: "p", text: `Therefore, it is premature to discuss it as a completed series or designate a viewing timetable; what is verified to date is the announcement of the studio partnership, the lead writer, and the formal commencement of script development.` },
    { type: "h3", text: `Another Global Feature Film in Active Development` },
    { type: "p", text: `In addition to the well-known **Miraculous: Ladybug & Cat Noir – The Movie**, Miraculous Corp announced in November 2025 the development of another global cinematic feature film project.` },
    { type: "p", text: `The announced creative team includes producer **John Cohen**, screenwriter **Matt Roller**, and **Jeremy Zag** participating as co-producer.` },
    { type: "p", text: `This project is entirely distinct from the earlier feature film, and the two should not be conflated simply because both present Ladybug and Cat Noir in a cinematic format. As of our verified reference data, this new production remained in active development, with no official release date yet revealed.` },
    { type: "p", text: `Thus, the world of Miraculous has expanded across multiple artistic expressions: from a feature film reimagining the origins, to television specials broadening the heroes' travels, short-form comedic shorts, an anime-inspired spin-off with a fresh team, and live-action adaptations and feature films currently in development. Each project provides a different way to engage with this universe, without implying that all of them constitute canonical installments of the primary television continuity.` },
    { type: "h2", text: `A Story Bigger Than Masks` },
    { type: "p", text: `What gives **Miraculous** its singular resonance is not merely that its dual protagonists possess superhuman abilities, but the nuanced manner in which their everyday lives intertwine with the secret realm in which they operate. Marinette and Adrien do not leave their feelings, vulnerabilities, and relationships behind when they put on their masks; rather, these human dimensions remain central to their decisions and adventures, while their contrasting alter egos infuse their narrative with tension, comedy, and romance.` },
    { type: "image", src: "data:image/webp;base64,", alt: "Marinette and Adrien: teenagers navigating life before their heroic callings.", caption: "Marinette and Adrien: teenagers navigating life before their heroic callings." },
    { type: "p", text: `Around this central premise, the Miraculous and Kwami lore imbues the universe with modularity and longevity, ensuring it does not remain confined to two characters or the adventures of Paris alone. Even as it expands into new mediums and entertainment formats, its core heartbeat remains unchanged: heroes confronting supernatural perils who are, at the same time, teenagers striving to understand themselves and their bonds with others. This delicate equilibrium between ordinary reality and soaring fantasy is what defines the soul of **Miraculous**, ensuring its universe continues to flourish and renew itself.` }
  ] as Section[],
};

export default article;
